There's no project file, so I'll give you the recommended approach directly.

## Lightest option: `TimeProvider` (built-in since .NET 8)

.NET 8+ ships `System.TimeProvider` — no NuGet packages, no custom interfaces needed. You inject it via a constructor parameter with a default:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In production** — nothing changes, callers keep using `new InvoiceCalculator()`.

**In tests** — use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(30)); // move time forward
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

Add the test NuGet package to your test project only:

```
dotnet add <TestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing
```

**Why this is the lightest path:**
- No custom interface or wrapper class to maintain
- `TimeProvider` is in the BCL — zero production dependencies
- The optional parameter means no DI container needed and no breaking changes to existing callers

